<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cluny Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">910</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Abbey of Cluny</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cluny Abbey was a Benedictine monastery at Cluny in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, founded in 910 by Duke William I of Aquitaine and placed directly under the pope, free from lay and episcopal control. Under abbots such as Odo, Odilo and Hugh it led a reform movement and headed hundreds of dependent houses across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its great church, the largest in Christendom until the new St Peter's in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, was largely demolished after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cluny-abbey.jpg" alt="Cluny Abbey" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cluny_-_B%C3%A2timents_abbatiaux_-_08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
